Project-specific classification rules:

- MOCK3-1 is decided by the comparison operator, not by prose. A criterion about age 65 is `met` only if the injected diff or file excerpt shows the actual comparison in `fareFor()` and that comparison puts 65 on the adult side. Quote the operator and the literal in your `evidence` field. A diff that only renames, reformats, adds a comment, or restates the ticket in a commit message is not implementation evidence — a criterion supported by nothing else is `unmet`.
- Boundary tests must pin the boundary. In the injected test oracle, a vitest case that exercises only ages either side of the line (e.g. 64 and 66) leaves the defect free to reappear: that is `partial`, and the gap is "no assertion at exactly 65". A green vitest run reported without the assertions that produced it does not upgrade anything — the pass/fail count is not evidence about which ages were checked.
- TypeScript proves nothing here. Types in this codeline cannot express "65 is adult"; a clean type-check or an added type annotation never moves a MOCK3-1 criterion toward `met`.
- MOCK3-2: if the injected evidence contains no departure-board code and no test touching one, do not infer that it exists elsewhere in mocka and do not credit the criterion. Where the oracle's criterion describes behaviour with no corresponding surface anywhere in the evidence, `unmet` (nothing implements it) and `untestable` (the evidence does not reach far enough to tell) are the only honest options — pick the one the evidence actually supports and say which in `gaps`.
- The routing fields above are pipeline context only. mocka has no browser-driven suite, so FORCE_LIGHTPANDA, FORCE_PLAYWRIGHT and routingDecision must not change any verdict, and must never cause you to invent an E2E or browser criterion that the story oracle does not record.
- The repository is small enough that the injected excerpts may be nearly the whole codeline. That makes silence informative, but it does not make it conclusive: still report absence as absence, with the file or diff you looked at named in `evidence`.

